A||||16||FAXKID||BEST PLUG WIRES||||1||
Z||000000||FAXKID||07-18-2001||10:01 PM||||With such high heat in the engine compartment<BR>what type of plug wires would be best on the 86 six gt?||||reg||1
Z||000001||RacinRob||07-18-2001||10:13 PM||||I have a set of super stock 8mm accel wires on my 85 gt and they are doing just fine, I've had them on there for about a year now and they look brand new. the great part is I paid 24.99 for a V-8 universal set so I have two extra wires just in case.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||07-18-2001||11:27 PM||||The best plug wires are the ones that are carefully routed and restrained.  Any "decent" brand will last for years. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||07-18-2001||11:29 PM||||I've had several friends that have problems with the Super stock wires.<BR>The 8.8 Accel wires are a much better wire<BR>and provide better heat protection.<BR>You can get them from Jegs for $49.99 and<BR>the part number is: 110 8879<P>MSD's 8.5's are also good wires.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-19-2001||12:25 AM||||What stu said is correct.<P>For high performance stuff I prefer Magnecor. they have an 8mm wire that fits into OE hardware without being damaged. Most 8mm wires are damaged by being stuffed into OE wire loom. OE routing is usually the best but you can hardly ever find clips to fit bigger wires and still fit the OE anchor points. Most aftermarket loom hardware looks awful or just doesn't last.<P>If/When I repalce the Borg Warner ones I'm using now I will put Magnecor on my DIS motor if they have them to fit. Hell, Maybe I'll make a set that actually fits right. Then I can get the boot angles the way I want them. They have the tools and materials for you to make total custom sets as well.<P>At anyrate, I'd recomend reading their material before spending money. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.magnecor.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.magnecor.com/</A> <P>So far the Borg Warner have been on for 2 years in the factory looms on the DIS motor. This places them as closer to the exhaust than I'd like but they haven't cared so I don't either. On the other hand they have lifetime warantee so maybe I'll just get another set free when it needs new ones....||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||07-19-2001||02:36 AM||||I have had the Accel 8.8mm for over 5 years on my present Fiero and had them on my first one for 6 years without any problems at all.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||07-19-2001||10:36 AM||||In this topic everybody will have their prefferences. I use Jacobs wires. About $60 direct from Jacobs Electronics and with life time warranty. This is a wire that you will feel the difference when you change from stock. Accel are good for the price but the ones that you build yourself could brake easily when taking off. At least that happen to me.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red & White (:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||07-20-2001||12:13 AM||||Summit sells Taylot Spiro-Pro, 8 mm, under $40 a set.  I've had mine since 93, and they haven't given me any trouble yet.  They come in red, too...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bruce||07-20-2001||12:55 AM||||I have the Taylor Vortex wires also. They do the job and are matched for the metal sleeves that protect the plugs from heat and arc-ing.||||reg||1
Z||000009||chester||07-20-2001||09:06 AM||||I have the Magnecor 10MM racing wires on my 2.8 and love 'em.  They come in the stock red color to boot.  <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||1
